    description: "Entropy is a measure of disorder or randomness within a system, and one of the most important ideas in all of physics. The second law of thermodynamics states that entropy in an isolated system never decreases on its own; left alone, things tend to move from order toward disorder, not the other way around. This is why an ice cube melts into a puddle but a puddle never spontaneously refreezes into a neat cube without outside help, and why a dropped glass shatters but never reassembles itself. On a small scale, this might seem like a fairly modest rule, but its implications stretch across the entire universe. Many scientists believe entropy gives time its forward direction, since events naturally unfold from more ordered states to less ordered ones, which is part of why we remember the past but not the future. Entropy also explains why no engine, however cleverly designed, can convert all of its input energy into useful work without losing some to heat and disorder. Despite sounding abstract, entropy shows up everywhere, from why rooms get messier over time to why stars eventually burn out."
